          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %em infinitesimal quantity
            is supposed to be a quantity that is infinitely small in size, yet not necessarily perfectly small (zero). An
            %em infinitesimal space
            is supposed to be a space whose extension is infinitely small, yet not necessarily perfectly small (pointlike).
          %p
            Infinitesimal objects have been conceived and used in one way or other for a long time, notably in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            emphasized the now familiar role of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} formal dual
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+scheme"} affine scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            with nilpotent ideals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J\subset R
            as
            %em infinitesimal thickenings
            of the formal dual of the quotient ring
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R/J
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+point"} infinitesimally thickened point
          %h3#formalization_in_synthetic_differential_geometry Formalization in synthetic differential geometry
          %p
            A proposal for formalizing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} abstract nonsense
            behind the notion of the
            %em infinitesimal
            such that these algebraic constructions become
            %em models
            for more general axioms was given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            in his 1967 lecture (see the references below).
          %p
            Lawvere observed that a simple yet powerful characterization of the notion of
            %em infinitesimal space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+hom"} inner hom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi D
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^D : \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{T}
            has a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            in question furthermore is equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lined+topos"} line object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            that plays the role of the
            %em real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            then a sensible notion of
            %em infinitesimal quantities
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is obtained when all
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \to R
            from infinitesimal spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are necessarily
            %em linear
            maps. This is now known as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock-Lawvere+axiom"} Kock-Lawvere axiom
            on
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lined+topos"} lined topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{T}, R)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{T}, R)
            is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
          %p
            The notion of infinitesimal object and infinitesimal space then makes sense in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} model
            for the axioms there will accordingly be concrete realizations of these infinitesimal objects.
          %h3#realizations_in_algebraic_geometry Realizations in algebraic geometry
          %p
            Notably, for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaves
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi CAlg
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k CAlg^{op}
            of that of commutative
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            (see for instance
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/SGM-final.pdf"} Kock-SGM, section 93
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} sheaf
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            takes place.
          %p
            Therefore the notion of infinitesimals in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            may be understood as being models of the general notion of infinitesimals in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            in context such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo =
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi CAlg
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} = Sh(k CAlg^{op})
            or similar.
          %p
            The vast majority of existing work on infinitesimals and infinitesimal neighbourhoods comes from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+differential+operators"} regular differential operators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystalline+cohomology"} crystalline cohomology
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+connection"} de Rham descent
          %p
            Similar infinitesimal thickenings also appear in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            of Kapranov, and in the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian categories
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"} quasicoherent sheaves
            in the work of Lunts and Rosenberg on regular
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+operator"} differential operator
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
          %h3#comparison_to_infinitesimals_in_nonstandard_analysis Comparison to infinitesimals in nonstandard analysis
          %p
            Another notion of infinitesimals has arisen in the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            in that they are all
            %em invertible
            (their inverses being “infinitely large”). Nevertheless, one can construct models of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            which, in addition to nilpotent infinitesimals, contain invertible infinitesimals; see for instance
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} MSIA, chapters VI and VII
          %p
            However, as pointed out in MSIA (intro. to Chapter VII), “there are some obvious differences.” The primary tool used in nonstandard analysis is a completely general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfer+principle"} transfer principle
          %p
            The construction of models for nonstandard analysis does, however, have a topos-theoretic description, using
            = succeed "s." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                filterpower
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/filterpower"} ?
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#atomic_object Atomic object
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_lawvere Definition (Lawvere)
            %p
              In a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is called
              %strong infinitesimal
              %strong atomic
              if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi D
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^D : C \to C
              for maps out of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              (i.e. the functor of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponentiation
              by
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
          %p
            An object which is infinitesimal atomic is small from the internal perspective of the category. In contrast, an object is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tiny+object"} tiny object
            when it is small from the external perspective, that is when the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(D,-):C\to Set
            into the category of sets preseves colimits.
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a local Grothendieck topos over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf 1
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is always internally small (infinitesimal atomic), since the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^{\mathbf 1}
            always has a right adjoint. But it is not necessarily a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tiny+object"} tiny object
            from the external perspective, since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\mathbf 1,-)
            need not preserve colimits. This happens in situations where it is not appropriate to view the terminal object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf 1
            in a categoy as a small object from the external perspective, for example when we are in a slice topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C/S
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong (intuitive interpretation)
            %p
              Here is how to think of what this definition means intuitively. For that, notice how maps out of an ordinary space fail to preserve
              = succeed "s:" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            %p
              for definiteness, consider the case of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              by spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}_i
              so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\coprod_{i,j} U_i \times_X U_j)
                    \\stackrel{\to}{\to}
                    (\coprod_i U_i)
                    \\to X
            %p
              as discussed in detail at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieve"} sieve
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is element of at least one of the covering spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              and that one obtains
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              by identifying the points in the covering spaces that correspond to the same one in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              Now let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              be any other space. We may assume here that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma,-] : T \to T
              at least preserves
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\coprod_{i,j} [\Sigma,U_i \times_X U_j])
                    \\stackrel{\to}{\to}
                    (\coprod_i [\Sigma,U_i])
                    \\to [\Sigma,X]
                    \\,.
            %p
              Now notice how this will in general fail to still be a
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\coprod_i [\Sigma,U_i])
                    \\to [\Sigma,X]
              would have to be an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
              factors through one of the covering spaces. The problem here is that in general the image of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
              may be
              %em larger
              than any of the
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            %p
              This is maybe most familiar in the context of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              the circle): the loop space of a cover of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is not in general a cover of the loop space.
            %p
              But suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              were infinitesimal. One thing that should mean is that there is no other space that is “effectively smaller” in some useful sense. For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              infinitesimal, we do expect that every map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
              can always be factored through at least one of the
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is so small, the image of a map out of it can never be too large.
            %p
              So only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              qualifies as having infinitesimal extension can the functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma,-]
              be expected to preserve colimits.
          %h3#formal_infinitesimal_space Formal infinitesimal space
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_formal_infinitesimal_space
              Definition
              %strong (formal infinitesimal space)
            %p
              An object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{T}, R)
              is called a
              %strong formally infinitesimal object
              if it is the algebra-spectrum of (what in the
              = succeed "-literature" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} sdg
              %em
                \-
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+point"} Weil algebra
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Spec
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta \simeq Spec_R(W)
                    \\,.
          %p Here
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo =
                      %mi R
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W = R \oplus J
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
                an
                = succeed "-finite" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Spec
                        %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mi R
                      %msub
                        %mi Alg
                        %mi 𝒯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi W
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊂
                      %msup
                        %mi R
                        %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec_R(W) := R Alg_{\mathcal{T}}(W,R) \subset R^W
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                of morphisms that respect the
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            All the spaces that are described as collection of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            infinitesimal neighbours are of this form. Infinitesimal spaces not of this form are germ-spaces (see the examples below). These violate the finite-dimensionality assumption on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#infinitesimal_intervals Infinitesimal intervals
          %p There are several different objects that one may think of as an infinitesimal interval.
          %p
            The smallest of them is often denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and sometimes called the
            %strong disembodied tangent vector
            or the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking"} walking
              tangent vector
            \.
          %p This is described in more detail at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+interval+object"}> infinitesimal interval object
              \.
          %p
            It is such that a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \to X
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the same as a choice of point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            and of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vector"} tangent vector
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in T_x X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            along the inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            produces the first-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet"} jet
            defined by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            at the point
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \hookrightarrow D \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            Accordingly, for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            there is a “slightly bigger” infinitesimal interval often denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_k \to \mathbb{R}
            produces the order-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet"} jet
            represented by this function at the given point.
          %p
            Still infinitesimal but bigger than all these is the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Λ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∩
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi ℝ
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda_0 := \cap_{0 \in U \subset \mathbb{R}} U
            of intersections of all neighbourhods of the origin of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Λ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda_0 \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            produces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/germ"} germ
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          %h3#the_standard_infinitesimal_interval The standard infinitesimal interval
          %h4#models Models
          %p
            The classical example of a realization of an infinitesimal object is in terms of what is (traditionally but undescriptively) called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                ring of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+number"} dual number
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dually
            in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} quantities
            on them, i.e. in terms of their would-be function algebras.
          %p
            For some real number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-t,t] \subset \mathbb{R}
            of length
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 t
            may be thought of as represented by functions on the whole real line
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p Precisely:
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              The (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+algebra"}> generalized smooth
              ) algebra of smooth functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty([-t,t])
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-t,t]
              is isomorphic to the quotient of the algebra of smooth functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbb{R})
              on all of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              by the functions that vanish on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-t,t]
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C^\infty([-t,t]) \simeq C^\infty(\mathbb{R})/\{f \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R})| \forall x \in [-t,t]: f(x) = 0\}
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              This is a corollary of the smooth version of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tietze+extension+theorem"} Tietze extension theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset \mathbb{R}^n
              a closed subset, every smooth function on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              extends to a smooth function on all of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %p
              See page 20 of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} MSIA
          %p As we think of the length of the interval shrinking to an infinitesimal value, the notion of derivative of functions is such that we want to say that the statement “a function vanishes on the infinitesimal interval” is equivalent to “a function vanishes at the origin and its first derivative there vanishes, too”. This in turn is usually equivalent (in a smooth context) to “a function is a square of a function that vanishes at the origin”.
          %p
            Accordingly, in a context where one considers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
            functions over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            – usually called
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            – that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msup
                    %mi Z
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[\epsilon] := k[Z]/Z^2
            which is the quotient of the polynomial ring in one variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            modulo the polynomial
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Z
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z^2
            %strong ring of dual numbers
            (where the term ‘dual’ historically refers to its being
            = succeed "-dimensional)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            subject to the relation that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon^2 = 0
          %p
            Similarly, in the smooth context of, for instance, Moerdijk–Reyes
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} Models for Smooth Infinitesimal Analysis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+algebra"} generalized smooth algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbb{R})/J^2
            obtained as the smooth functions on the real line modulo squares of functions that vanish at the origin.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_the_1dimensional_infinitesimal_space Definition (the 1-dimensional infinitesimal space)
            %p
              In the context of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+algebra"} generalized smooth algebra
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              whose function algebra is the quotient
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C^\infty(D) := C^\infty(\mathbb{R})/\{x^2\}
            %p
              of all functions on the real line, modulo those that are a product with the function
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ↦
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto x^2
          %p
            This does reproduce the above ring of dual numbers due to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+lemma"} Hadamard lemma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R})
            a smooth function, there exists a smooth function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R})
            such that for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \mathbb{R}
            we have
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi x
                    %mi g
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(x) = g(0) + x g'(x) + x^2 h(x)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^2
          %p
            See pages 19-20 of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} MSIA
          %p
            In this dual generators-and-relations description, the infinitesimal interval is very familiar in many mathematically less sophisticated contexts. It prevails for instance in the basic physics textbook treatment since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isaac+Newton"} Isaac Newton
            up to this day.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sophus+Lie"} Sophus Lie
            is famously quoted as having said that he found many of his famous insights by such “synthetic reasoning” and only a lack of proper formalization prevented him from writing them up in this way instead of in the more wide-spread way of differential calculus.
          %h4#axiomatics Axiomatics
          %p
            More generally, one may abstract the above properties of concrete realizations of the infinitesimal interval such as to get such a notion in an arbitrary suitable context. A suitable context for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            is any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            equipped with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            Using the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            we may speak of both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            as if they were
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} generalized element
          #FirstOrderInfinitesimalInterval.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{T}, R)
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+interval+object"} infinitesimal interval object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              of all those elements whose square is 0.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D = \{x \in R | x^2 = 0\}
          %p
            It may be helpful to recall that in terms of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
            of
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R \stackrel{(-)^2}{\to} R
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R \stackrel{}{\to} 0 \stackrel{}{\to} R
                  \\,.
          %p
            For with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : U \to D
            any morphism embodying a generalized element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            identifies this uniquely with a morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to R
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to R \stackrel{(-)^2}{\to} R
            is the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            with domain of definition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            \:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo =
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots = U \to 0 \to R
          %h3#the_cartesian_product_of_infinitesimal_intervals The cartesian product of infinitesimal intervals
          %p
            This works analogously to how the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube"} cube
            is the
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^k
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-1,1]
            with itself.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The
              = succeed "-fold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^k
              of the first-order infinitesimal interval
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FirstOrderInfinitesimalInterval"}
              = succeed "-cube\u201D." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %p
              By the discussion at
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+algebra"} smooth algebra
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi k
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C^\infty(D^k) \simeq C^\infty(D)^{\otimes^k} \simeq (\mathbb{R}[\epsilon]/(\epsilon^2))^{\otimes^k}
                    \\,.
          %h3#the_dimensional_infinitesimal_disk
            The
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\in \mathbb{N}
              the
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mi k
