          %h2#references References
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimals"} infinitesimals
            goes back to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leibniz"} Leibniz
          %ul
            %li#Cohen83
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Cohen"}> Hermann Cohen
              ,
              %em Das Prinzip der Infinitesimal-Methode und seine Geschichte
              , Berlin 1883. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.gleichsatz.de/b-u-t/archiv/Cohen/hc-infinit1.html"}> html
              )
          %p Nowadays infinitesimal spaces and their properties were familiar in all those areas of mathematics where spaces are characterized by the algebras of functions on them.
          %p It was in a seminal lecture
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              %em Categorical Dynamics
              lectures at the University of Chicago (1967)
          %p reproduced in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topos theoretic methods in Geometry
          %p
            that the proposal was made to axiomatize the properties of infinitesimal objects by making use of the fact that they are supposed to be objects of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
          %p
            It was from this insight that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            was eventually developed.
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Outline of synthetic differential geometry
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.acsu.buffalo.edu/~wlawvere/downloadlist.html"}> web
                )
          %p
            This is a classical case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+abstract+nonsense"} general abstract nonsense
            used to understand a subtle situation.
          %p
            A summary and discussion of the axiomatically defined standard infinitesimal objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_k(n)
            is in section 1.2 of
          %ul
            %li
              Anders Kock,
              %em Synthetic Geometry of Manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/SGM-final.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %h3#atomic_spaces Atomic spaces
          %p
            The proposal to call objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,-]
            has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amazing+right+adjoint"} amazing right adjoint
            “atomic objects” is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              %em Toposes of Laws of Motion
          %p and repeated in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              %em Open problems in topos theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://cheng.staff.shef.ac.uk/pssl88/lawvere.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Details on the right adjoint to the exponentiation functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^X
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            an infinitesimal object are in appendix 4 of
          %ul
            %li
              Moerdijk, Reyes,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} Models for Smooth Infinitesimal Analysis
          %h3#formally_infinitesimal_spaces Formally infinitesimal spaces
          %p
            For formal infinitesimal objects and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+point"} Weil algebras
            see
          %p section I.16 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              %em Synthetic differential geometry
              (
              %a{:href => ""}> pdf
              )
          %p and chapter I, section 4 and chapter II, theorem 1.13 and onwards in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} Models for Smooth Infinitesimal Analysis
          %p
